    "body": "<p>An Arctic soundscape is a soundscape of ice, wind, and brief, intense life. Sea ice groans, cracks, and booms as it shifts, a geophony of enormous low-frequency events that can travel far through ice and water. Wind over tundra and snow adds a steady hiss, and meltwater streams add summer trickles. Biophony is seasonal: migratory birds arrive in a rush of song in the short summer, seals call under the ice, and whales, including bowheads and belugas, sing and call in ice-covered waters where their low and mid-frequency voices carry for kilometres. In winter, the soundscape thins to wind and ice alone, and the silence between events is profound. Arctic soundscapes are changing measurably as sea ice retreats: open water brings more wave noise and more shipping anthrophony into waters that were once ice-quiet.</p><h4>Science proof</h4><p>Science proof: Arctic acoustic monitoring documents sea-ice sounds, seasonal bird and marine-mammal vocal activity, and the increase in shipping noise as ice-free seasons lengthen. Under-ice seal and whale calls are recorded by hydrophones deployed through the ice, and their seasonal patterns track migration and breeding. The link between ice loss and soundscape change is published across Arctic monitoring programmes.</p><h4>Math proof</h4><p>Math proof: if sea-ice cover falls from 100 percent to 60 percent of a study area, the open-water share rises from 0 percent to 40 percent. If wave noise level rises by 3 dB for each 20 percent of open water, the rise for 40 percent open water is (40 / 20) x 3 = 6.0 dB. The demo prints open water 40 percent and wave-noise rise 6.0 dB, plus the intensity ratio for 6 dB, 10^(6/10) = 3.98 times, shown as 4.0.</p><h4>Soundscape layer(s)</h4><p>biophony; geophony</p><h4>Source</h4><p>Published Arctic soundscape and sea-ice acoustics literature; figures as published October 2026</p>",
